<section class="research-demo" data-demo="{{ include.demo }}">
  <p class="folio-eyebrow">Try the intuition</p>
  {% if include.demo == 'logits' %}
    <h2>How steep is the score curve?</h2>
    <p>In the approximation z(r) = μ − α log(r), α controls how quickly scores fall with rank. Here μ is fixed at 5.</p>
    <div data-demo-controls hidden>
      <label for="slope">Slope α <output data-demo-value for="slope">1.5</output></label
      ><input id="slope" type="range" min="0.2" max="2" step="0.1" value="1.5">
    </div>
    <svg viewBox="0 0 520 220" role="img" aria-labelledby="logit-chart-title">
      <title id="logit-chart-title">Illustrative ordered logit scores as a function of rank</title><path class="demo-axis" d="M45 20V180H500"></path><path data-demo-curve d="M45.00,30.00 L63.20,66.21 L85.95,81.81 L176.95,106.53 L495.45,133.62"></path><text x="45" y="207">Rank 1</text><text x="425" y="207">Rank 100</text><text x="5" y="18">Score</text>
    </svg>
    <p data-demo-explanation aria-live="polite">Higher slopes produce a sharper fall in scores.</p>
    <p class="demo-note">An illustration of the paper’s equation, not measured logits or a model performance predictor.</p>
  {% elsif include.demo == 'smoothing' %}
    <h2>Borrow a little evidence.</h2>
    <p>A local estimate and a neighboring context suggest different next-token probabilities. Change how much evidence to borrow.</p>
    <div data-demo-controls hidden>
      <label for="borrow">Neighbor weight <output data-demo-value for="borrow">40%</output></label
      ><input id="borrow" type="range" min="0" max="100" step="5" value="40">
    </div>
    <div class="demo-distributions">
      <div>
        <span>Token A</span>
        <div class="demo-bar"><span data-probability="0" style="width: 54%"></span></div>
        <output data-probability-value="0">54%</output>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span>Token B</span>
        <div class="demo-bar"><span data-probability="1" style="width: 26%"></span></div>
        <output data-probability-value="1">26%</output>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span>Token C</span>
        <div class="demo-bar"><span data-probability="2" style="width: 20%"></span></div>
        <output data-probability-value="2">20%</output>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="demo-note">
      Toy distributions: local = (70%, 20%, 10%); neighbor = (30%, 35%, 35%). This shows linear interpolation, not the paper’s estimator selection or
      evaluation.
    </p>
  {% elsif include.demo == 'workflow' %}
    <h2>Three experiences. One procedure.</h2>
    <p>Different objects can share reusable steps, while environment knowledge and cautions adapt as experience accumulates.</p>
    <div class="workflow-example"><span>Heat mug</span><span>Heat bowl</span><span>Heat plate</span></div>
    <details open>
      <summary>The shared workflow</summary>
      <p>Find the object → use the heating appliance → retrieve the heated object → place it at the requested destination.</p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>What changes with experience?</summary>
      <p>
        Environment knowledge records useful regularities. Procedural steps capture what worked. Failure guidance preserves cautions from unsuccessful
        attempts when the update policy uses them.
      </p>
    </details>
    <p class="demo-note">A schematic example of the representation, not a live agent run.</p>
  {% endif %}
</section>
